<file_context path="src/mcp.js">

# application_context The caller's own tools, served to the agent as an MCP server

`session/prompt` carries `{sessionId, prompt, _meta}` — verified against the SDK's
generated types, including the v2 draft. There is no `tools` field and there is not
going to be one: an ACP agent runs its **own** tool loop, and the protocol's answer
to "where do tools come from" is `mcpServers`, declared when the session opens.

So a caller's OpenAI `tools` cannot be served by passing anything down. They can
only be served by **becoming a tool server**, which is what this file is: a small
MCP server, in-process, on the bridge's own HTTP port, whose tool list is whatever
the current request declared. The agent connects to it like any other MCP server
and never learns the tools belong to whoever is on the other end of the completion.

# decision A tool call is PARKED, not answered {#park}

An MCP tool call has to be answered, and only the caller can answer it — over HTTP,
in a later request, in OpenAI's shape. So the JSON-RPC response is held open, the
completion returns `finish_reason: "tool_calls"`, and the next request carrying
`role: "tool"` results resolves it.

**The ACP turn never ends.** It sits inside a tool call, which is a perfectly
ordinary thing for a turn to be doing, and when the result arrives the agent picks
up exactly where it was — mid-plan, with everything it had read still in hand —
rather than re-planning from a summary. That is the whole reason for the
complexity; the alternative (end the turn, feed the result as a new prompt) is
simpler and throws away the state the caller paid for.

What it costs, and what pays for it:

- the turn's event sink must outlive the request that started it, buffering
  whatever the agent says while nobody is attached (`makeSink` in server.js);
- the conversation stays **busy** while suspended, so a second turn cannot start on
  a session whose first one is merely waiting. `matchKey` hands a suspended turn
  back regardless of `server.busy`, because the next request is not a second turn —
  it is the rest of this one;
- a parked call has a deadline. A caller that never comes back would otherwise
  leave an agent process waiting forever on a subscription that is still being
  paid for.

# invariant A handed-over call id names only its live suspended turn {#live_call_index}

`ToolBridge` mints the OpenAI `tool_call_id`, owns the parked call, and therefore
owns the only call-id-to-conversation index. An id enters that index only when the
parked call is handed to the HTTP caller and the conversation is recorded as
pending. It leaves when the result resolves the call, the tool deadline fires, the
bench closes, or `SessionStore` clears that pending turn for any other reason.

`conversation(callId)` is a read-only lookup and validates the indexed entry
against the bench's still-parked call before returning it. It can never resolve an
id to a closed conversation or resurrect a turn whose pending state was cleared.
Do not build a second registry in the HTTP or session layer: it would drift from
`resolve`, which is the authority on whether the call still exists.

# invariant A bench keeps one canonical tool surface {#stable_tools}

`toolFingerprint` converts Chat or Responses function definitions to MCP form,
sorts tool names and object keys recursively, and identifies the surface fixed at
session creation. `setTools` accepts semantically identical definitions but throws
400 `tool_set_changed` for a changed name or schema. Reordering definitions or JSON
keys is not a change.

`setEnabled(false)` implements `tool_choice: none` per turn without destroying
that fixed surface: `tools/list` returns an empty list and `tools/call` rejects even
a name the agent cached earlier. `setEnabled(true)` restores `auto` for a later
turn. This switch affects caller-owned tools only, never configured agent-owned MCP.

# invariant The path token is the MCP credential {#no_key}

The agent is a child of this process, reached over loopback. There is no API key on
the bridge; the unguessable token in the path is the credential, and it names one
conversation's tools. The MCP route is resolved before ordinary API routing so the
agent needs nothing from the OpenAI caller's request headers.

Historically, putting the route under the now-removed API-key check answered every
agent connection 401. It failed **silently**: caller tools never appeared, the
agent reported "no such tool is available", and nothing in the log said why. That
incident cost two live subscription turns to find, and is why a test pins
token-only reachability and route order.

`log()` on every MCP request exists for the same reason: whether the agent ARRIVES
is the first question worth asking when tools do not show up, and reasoning about
someone else's MCP client is not an answer.

# system_invariant Transport: stateless streamable HTTP {#transport}

The session is named by the token in the URL, so no `Mcp-Session-Id` is issued and
none is expected. Requests are answered as plain JSON. There is no server-initiated
stream, so `GET` is refused with 405 — which the spec allows in as many words for a
server that does not offer one. A notification (no `id`) is answered 202 with no
body.

`initialize` echoes back the client's `protocolVersion` when it sent one. A
stricter answer buys nothing: the surface here is `tools/list` and `tools/call`,
which have not changed across the versions in the wild.

# verification_and_unit_test_criteria

Both shipped adapters were verified live, end to end, on 2026-08-13 — not with a
stub, because a stub proves only that the test agrees with itself:

| | claude-agent-acp 0.66.0 | codex-acp 1.1.14 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| tool appears as | `mcp__acp2api-client-tools__company_holiday` | `mcp.acp2api-client-tools.company_holiday` |
| handshake | initialize → notifications/initialized → tools/list → tools/call | same |
| completion | `finish_reason: "tool_calls"` | same |
| after the result | the same turn finished from it | the same turn finished from it |

The probe tool returned a value that exists nowhere else (`MERIDIAN-77`), so the
answer could not have come from anywhere but the caller's result.

- a caller's tools are offered to the agent, and a call comes back as tool_calls
- the result the caller sends back reaches the same turn, which then finishes
- every way a pending turn ends removes its handed-over call ids from lookup
- a tool result for a call nobody is waiting on is refused, not run as a new turn
- server.tools off keeps the old behaviour: no MCP server is attached
- tool_choice none disables discovery and stale cached calls, and auto re-enables them
- a changed tool fingerprint is rejected while semantic reordering is accepted
- the MCP endpoint is reachable with only its path token

The fixture acts as a real MCP client over HTTP for these — `USETOOL` in
test/fixtures/fake-agent.js.

# scope_boundary What this is not

Not a general MCP server: it serves exactly the tools of one conversation's current
request, and `tools/list` is the only discovery it offers. No resources, no
prompts, no sampling, no server-initiated notifications — including
`tools/list_changed`, which is why a caller is expected to send a stable tool set
for the life of a conversation. An agent that has already listed is not told the
list changed.

</file_context>
